WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.031
Picture a small town.

2
00:00:04.031 --> 00:00:08.089
It has one town hall, where people go to register a new address.

3
00:00:08.089 --> 00:00:11.893
Nobody builds a second one, and everybody knows where it is.

4
00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:19.713
The town has a corner shop, and the shop's order program needs a town hall of its own: the price list.

5
00:00:19.713 --> 00:00:23.120
Every part of the program asks it what things cost.

6
00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:24.923
A loaf of bread: 450 cents.

7
00:00:24.923 --> 00:00:26.793
There should be exactly one.

8
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:32.127
An object like that, one instance that everyone shares, is called a singleton.

9
00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:36.185
Now here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

10
00:00:36.185 --> 00:00:38.643
Making one of something sounds trivial.

11
00:00:38.643 --> 00:00:40.786
If there isn't one yet, build one.

12
00:00:40.786 --> 00:00:42.425
Isn't a null check enough?

13
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:46.427
Here's the version most of us write first.

14
00:00:46.427 --> 00:00:53.958
Loading the price list means reading the supplier's price file, which is slow, so we wait until somebody first asks.

15
00:00:53.958 --> 00:00:55.191
That's called lazy.

16
00:00:55.191 --> 00:00:58.113
If the field is null, build one and store it.

17
00:00:58.113 --> 00:00:59.541
Kotlin looks the same.

18
00:01:00.467 --> 00:01:01.530
Back to the town.

19
00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:05.970
Before the hall exists, there's an empty plot of land set aside for it.

20
00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:09.660
The first builder who finds the plot empty builds the hall.

21
00:01:10.467 --> 00:01:11.317
Let's map it.

22
00:01:11.317 --> 00:01:13.869
The plot is the field, called instance.

23
00:01:13.869 --> 00:01:15.504
An empty plot means null.

24
00:01:15.504 --> 00:01:17.860
The town hall is the one price list.

25
00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:25.711
And the builders are threads: paths of work that run at the same time, one for each customer order the shop is handling.

26
00:01:26.633 --> 00:01:27.923
Let's slow it down.

27
00:01:27.923 --> 00:01:29.280
Thread A asks first.

28
00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:31.181
It looks at the plot: empty.

29
00:01:31.181 --> 00:01:34.575
So it starts building, and building takes a while.

30
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:37.556
While A is still building, thread B asks.

31
00:01:37.556 --> 00:01:38.632
What does B see?

32
00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:43.780
Empty.

33
00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:46.099
A hasn't stored anything yet.

34
00:01:46.099 --> 00:01:48.099
So B starts building too.

35
00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:50.620
A finishes and stores its price list.

36
00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:54.006
Then B finishes, and stores another one right over it.

37
00:01:54.006 --> 00:01:58.645
Two threads, two price lists, and nobody can say which one a caller holds.

38
00:01:58.867 --> 00:02:00.887
And here the town picture breaks.

39
00:02:00.887 --> 00:02:05.602
Two real builders at an empty plot would see each other and agree who builds.

40
00:02:05.602 --> 00:02:07.806
Threads can't see each other at all.

41
00:02:07.806 --> 00:02:09.581
Each one sees only the field.

42
00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:15.161
This is a check-then-act race: two separate steps, and another thread slips in between.

43
00:02:16.067 --> 00:02:17.583
So how bad can it get?

44
00:02:17.583 --> 00:02:26.408
The lesson's LazyRaceDemo lets eight threads ask at the same moment, with a constructor that takes 20 ms, like a slow file read.

45
00:02:26.408 --> 00:02:28.545
How many price lists get built?

46
00:02:31.733 --> 00:02:32.159
Eight.

47
00:02:32.159 --> 00:02:36.203
The constructor ran 8 times, and callers saw 8 instances.

48
00:02:36.203 --> 00:02:40.246
Eight threads asked for the one price list and got eight.

49
00:02:40.246 --> 00:02:41.381
So much for one.

50
00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:44.647
In the town, that's eight town halls on one plot.

51
00:02:44.647 --> 00:02:45.517
Very generous.

52
00:02:45.517 --> 00:02:50.367
Register your new address in one, and the other seven have never heard of you.

53
00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:52.281
So the point really is this.

54
00:02:52.281 --> 00:02:54.682
A null check can't promise one instance.

55
00:02:54.682 --> 00:02:57.383
Something has to make the other threads wait.

56
00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:01.014
Java gives us three good tools for that.

57
00:03:01.014 --> 00:03:02.779
The first is the simplest.

58
00:03:02.779 --> 00:03:11.600
If the object is cheap, or needed at startup anyway, like the counter that hands out order numbers, use an enum with one constant.

59
00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:17.911
The JVM, the program that runs your Java and Kotlin code, creates that constant exactly once.

60
00:03:18.633 --> 00:03:23.581
The second is for an expensive object you want lazily, like our price list.

61
00:03:23.581 --> 00:03:27.275
Put the instance in a small nested class, called Holder.

62
00:03:27.275 --> 00:03:32.883
The JVM sets up a class the first time anyone uses it, and it does that exactly once.

63
00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:37.213
Think of the JVM as a strict doorkeeper at Holder's door.

64
00:03:37.213 --> 00:03:40.572
Thread A arrives first, and goes in to run the setup.

65
00:03:40.572 --> 00:03:44.628
Thread B arrives a moment later, and waits politely at the door.

66
00:03:44.833 --> 00:03:46.667
A builds the one price list.

67
00:03:46.667 --> 00:03:50.729
Only then does the door open, and B gets the very same object.

68
00:03:50.729 --> 00:03:54.987
Nobody pushes past this doorkeeper, not even a thread in a hurry.

69
00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:00.213
The demo agrees: the constructor ran once, and every caller saw one instance.

70
00:04:00.213 --> 00:04:05.030
After that, every call is a plain read of a static field, with no waiting.

71
00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:10.303
That's the holder idiom: a tiny class whose only job is to hold the one instance.

72
00:04:11.033 --> 00:04:17.865
The third tool is for a lazy instance field, one per object, where a holder can't help: double-checked locking.

73
00:04:17.865 --> 00:04:19.958
Read the field once, with no lock.

74
00:04:19.958 --> 00:04:26.605
Only if it's still null, take a lock, and check again, because another thread may have won while you waited.

75
00:04:26.833 --> 00:04:28.509
And mark the field volatile.

76
00:04:28.509 --> 00:04:33.357
That makes a write to the field visible to every thread that reads it afterwards.

77
00:04:33.357 --> 00:04:38.864
Leave volatile out, and another thread can see a price list whose contents it can't see yet.

78
00:04:39.767 --> 00:04:41.156
So here's the rule.

79
00:04:41.156 --> 00:04:44.520
A plain null check can't promise one instance.

80
00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:46.933
Let the JVM or a lock promise it.

81
00:04:47.133 --> 00:04:50.419
Kotlin moves two of these tools into the language.

82
00:04:50.419 --> 00:04:59.157
An object declaration is a singleton made on first access, and by lazy is double-checked locking, written once and correctly for you.

83
00:05:00.067 --> 00:05:00.977
One guardrail.

84
00:05:00.977 --> 00:05:07.283
Remember the town hall's notice board: a note pinned there for one visitor gets read by the next.

85
00:05:07.283 --> 00:05:13.655
Even a safe singleton is one global thing, and state a test leaves in it leaks into the next test.

86
00:05:14.367 --> 00:05:17.270
So the best singleton is often one you don't write.

87
00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:22.679
Make one price list where the program is put together, and pass it in to everyone who needs it.

88
00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:25.693
So, isn't a null check enough?

89
00:05:25.693 --> 00:05:25.903
No.

90
00:05:25.903 --> 00:05:30.578
The check and the build are two steps, and threads slip in between.

91
00:05:30.578 --> 00:05:34.485
Let the JVM's doorkeeper or a lock make the others wait.

92
00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:35.529
Quick check.

93
00:05:35.529 --> 00:05:43.823
You delete the slow sleep from the constructor, run the racy version three times, and it prints: constructor ran 1 time.

94
00:05:43.823 --> 00:05:45.067
Is the race fixed?

95
00:05:48.233 --> 00:05:48.427
No.

96
00:05:48.427 --> 00:05:53.076
The gap between the check and the act just shrank to a few microseconds.

97
00:05:53.076 --> 00:05:57.725
A real price file is slow, so the race comes back when the shop is busy.
